eorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-euclid"><data key="title">Euclidean algorithm</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-euclid", "topicId": "math-integers", "title": "Euclidean algorithm", "latex": "\\gcd(a,b)=\\gcd(b,a-qb)", "plain": "Euclidean algorithm under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "a,b", "meaning": "integers with b nonzero", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "q", "meaning": "integer quotient", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Use integer division a=qb+r with 0≤r&lt;|b|."], "derivation": ["Common divisors divide each integer combination.", "Each remainder decreases until zero."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "a=84,b=30", "steps": ["84=2*30+24", "30=24+6 and 24=4*6"], "result": "gcd(84,30)=6", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Over arbitrary rings a division algorithm need not exist."], "pitfalls": ["Over arbitrary rings a division algorithm need not exist.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-bezout"><data key="title">Bézout identity over integers</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-bezout", "topicId": "math-integers", "title": "Bézout identity over integers", "latex": "\\gcd(a,b)=ua+vb", "plain": "Bézout identity over integers under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "a,b", "meaning": "integers not both zero", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "u,v", "meaning": "integer coefficients", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The gcd is chosen positive."], "derivation": ["Back substitute the Euclidean remainders or take the least positive ideal element."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "a=84,b=30", "steps": ["6=30-(84-2*30)"], "result": "6=(-1)*84+3*30", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A gcd in a general integral domain need not be an integer-style linear combination."], "pitfalls": ["A gcd in a general integral domain need not be an integer-style linear combination.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-modular-inverse"><data key="title">Criterion for a modular inverse</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-modular-inverse", "topicId": "math-congruences", "title": "Criterion for a modular inverse", "latex": "a^{-1}\\pmod n\\text{ exists}\\iff\\gcd(a,n)=1", "plain": "Criterion for a modular inverse under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "a", "meaning": "integer residue representative", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "n", "meaning": "integer modulus greater than one", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Equality is in the quotient ring Z/nZ."], "derivation": ["Bézout supplies au+nv=1.", "Conversely an inverse makes every common divisor divide1."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "a=3,n=7", "steps": ["3*5=15=1+2*7"], "result": "Inverse of3 modulo7 is5", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["2 is nonzero modulo6 but has no inverse."], "pitfalls": ["2 is nonzero modulo6 but has no inverse.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-geometric-sum"><data key="title">Finite geometric sum</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-geometric-sum", "topicId": "math-induction", "title": "Finite geometric sum", "latex": "\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}r^k=\\frac{1-r^n}{1-r}", "plain": "Finite geometric sum under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "n", "meaning": "positive integer", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "r", "meaning": "real or complex scalar", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["For the displayed quotient r≠1; at r=1 the sum is n."], "derivation": ["Multiply the finite sum by1-r and cancel adjacent terms."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "r=2,n=4", "steps": ["1+2+4+8=(1-16)/(1-2)"], "result": "15", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["An infinite sum requires |r|&lt;1; a finite identity does not justify an infinite limit."], "pitfalls": ["An infinite sum requires |r|&lt;1; a finite identity does not justify an infinite limit.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-factor"><data key="title">Polynomial factor theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-factor", "topicId": "math-polynomials", "title": "Polynomial factor theorem", "latex": "p(a)=0\\iff p(x)=(x-a)q(x)", "plain": "Polynomial factor theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "p,q", "meaning": "polynomials over a field", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "field element", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Evaluation and division take place over the same field."], "derivation": ["Divide p by x-a; the constant remainder is p(a)."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "p=x²-1,a=1", "steps": ["x²-1=(x-1)(x+1)"], "result": "q=x+1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A polynomial function on a finite field may vanish everywhere without being the zero formal polynomial."], "pitfalls": ["A polynomial function on a finite field may vanish everywhere without being the zero formal polynomial.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-rank-nullity"><data key="title">Rank–nullity</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-rank-nullity", "topicId": "math-linear-maps", "title": "Rank–nullity", "latex": "\\dim V=\\dim\\ker T+\\dim\\operatorname{im}T", "plain": "Rank–nullity under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "V", "meaning": "finite-dimensional vector space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "T", "meaning": "linear map V→W", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["V and W have a common scalar field; finite dimension is essential for integer subtraction."], "derivation": ["Extend a kernel basis to a basis of V.", "Images of the added vectors form an image basis."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "T:R³→R², T(x,y,z)=(x+y,z)", "steps": ["Kernel is span(1,-1,0); image is R²"], "result": "3=1+2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A nonlinear map has no rank-nullity theorem in this form."], "pitfalls": ["A nonlinear map has no rank-nullity theorem in this form.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-solvability"><data key="title">Affine solution fiber</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-solvability", "topicId": "math-linear-maps", "title": "Affine solution fiber", "latex": "\\{x:Ax=b\\}=x_0+\\ker A", "plain": "Affine solution fiber under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "b", "meaning": "target vector", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x0", "meaning": "one solution", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["A solution x0 must exist before the affine fiber formula is used."], "derivation": ["Subtract Ax=Ax0 and use linearity."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=[1 1],b=3", "steps": ["x0=(3,0); add t(1,-1)"], "result": "All solutions are(3+t,-t)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["If b is outside the range, there is no affine solution fiber."], "pitfalls": ["If b is outside the range, there is no affine solution fiber.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-determinant"><data key="title">Multiplicativity of determinant</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-determinant", "topicId": "math-determinants", "title": "Multiplicativity of determinant", "latex": "\\det(AB)=\\det A\\det B", "plain": "Multiplicativity of determinant under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A,B", "meaning": "square matrices of the same size", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Entries lie in a commutative field or ring."], "derivation": ["Use the alternating multilinear characterization on the columns of the product."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=diag(2,3),B=diag(5,7)", "steps": ["detA=6,detB=35; AB=diag(10,21)"], "result": "detAB=210", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A determinant is not the sum of eigenvalues; that is the trace."], "pitfalls": ["A determinant is not the sum of eigenvalues; that is the trace.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-spectral-matrix"><data key="title">Finite-dimensional spectral theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-spectral-matrix", "topicId": "math-eigenvalues", "title": "Finite-dimensional spectral theorem", "latex": "A=U\\Lambda U^*,\\quad U^*U=I", "plain": "Finite-dimensional spectral theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "complex normal matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "U", "meaning": "unitary matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Λ", "meaning": "diagonal matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["A*A=AA*; for self-adjoint A, eigenvalues are real."], "derivation": ["Schur triangularization plus normality eliminates off-diagonal entries."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=[[2,1],[1,2]]", "steps": ["Normalized eigenvectors(1,1)/√2 and(1,-1)/√2 have eigenvalues3 and1"], "result": "An orthonormal eigenbasis exists", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["The Jordan block[[1,1],[0,1]] is not normal and has no eigenbasis."], "pitfalls": ["The Jordan block[[1,1],[0,1]] is not normal and has no eigenbasis.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Exact selected theorem statement and surrounding argument inspected; not all source proofs audited.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-svd"><data key="title">Singular-value decomposition</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-svd", "topicId": "math-svd", "title": "Singular-value decomposition", "latex": "A=U\\Sigma V^*", "plain": "Singular-value decomposition under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "m×n real or complex matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "U,V", "meaning": "unitary matrices", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Σ", "meaning": "nonnegative diagonal rectangular matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Finite dimensions and Euclidean/Hermitian inner products are fixed."], "derivation": ["Diagonalize A*A.", "Normalize nonzero images to obtain left singular vectors and complete bases."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=diag(3,0)", "steps": ["A*A=diag(9,0)"], "result": "Singular values are3 and0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Singular values are nonnegative and are not generally the complex eigenvalues."], "pitfalls": ["Singular values are nonnegative and are not generally the complex eigenvalues.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-least-squares"><data key="title">Normal equations and projection</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-least-squares", "topicId": "math-least-squares", "title": "Normal equations and projection", "latex": "A^*Ax=A^*b", "plain": "Normal equations and projection under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "b", "meaning": "target vector", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "least-squares minimizer", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Euclidean norm; uniqueness follows if A has full column rank."], "derivation": ["Differentiate the squared residual or impose residual orthogonality to ranA."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=(1,1)^T,b=(1,3)^T", "steps": ["2x=4"], "result": "Unique minimizer x=2, residual(-1,1)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Forming A*A can square the condition number; QR/SVD is often numerically preferable."], "pitfalls": ["Forming A*A can square the condition number; QR/SVD is often numerically preferable.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-pseudoinverse"><data key="title">Minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-pseudoinverse", "topicId": "math-svd", "title": "Minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution", "latex": "x=A^+b", "plain": "Minimum-norm pseudoinverse solution under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A+", "meaning": "Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "b", "meaning": "target vector", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The claim concerns the minimum-norm member among least-squares solutions."], "derivation": ["Invert nonzero singular values and set null singular coordinates to zero."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=[1 1],b=2", "steps": ["The solution fiber is(1+t,1-t); squared norm=2+2t²"], "result": "Minimum norm at x=(1,1)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Small singular values amplify noise; a pseudoinverse is not a regularization guarantee."], "pitfalls": ["Small singular values amplify noise; a pseudoinverse is not a regularization guarantee.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-cayley-hamilton"><data key="title">Cayley–Hamilton theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-cayley-hamilton", "topicId": "math-eigenvalues", "title": "Cayley–Hamilton theorem", "latex": "p_A(A)=0", "plain": "Cayley–Hamilton theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "n×n matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "pA", "meaning": "characteristic polynomial det(tI-A)", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Finite square matrix over a commutative field."], "derivation": ["Use polynomial adjugate identity and compare coefficients before substituting A."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=diag(2,3), p=t²-5t+6", "steps": ["Each diagonal entry satisfies p(2)=p(3)=0"], "result": "A²-5A+6I=0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Substituting a matrix into an identity with noncommuting coefficients needs care."], "pitfalls": ["Substituting a matrix into an identity with noncommuting coefficients needs care.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-lagrange"><data key="title">Lagrange theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-lagrange", "topicId": "math-groups", "title": "Lagrange theorem", "latex": "|G|=|H|[G:H]", "plain": "Lagrange theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "G", "meaning": "finite group", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "H", "meaning": "subgroup", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "[G", "meaning": "H]:number of left cosets", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["H is a subgroup, not an arbitrary subset."], "derivation": ["Cosets partition G and multiplication gives bijections H→gH."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "G=S3,H={e,(12)}", "steps": ["There are3 cosets each of size2"], "result": "6=2*3", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A divisor of group order need not occur as subgroup order in every finite group."], "pitfalls": ["A divisor of group order need not occur as subgroup order in every finite group.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-isomorphism"><data key="title">First isomorphism theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-isomorphism", "topicId": "math-groups", "title": "First isomorphism theorem", "latex": "G/\\ker\\varphi\\cong\\operatorname{im}\\varphi", "plain": "First isomorphism theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "φ", "meaning": "group homomorphism", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "kerφ", "meaning": "normal kernel", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The map preserves the group operation."], "derivation": ["Send the coset g kerφ to φ(g); verify well-definedness and bijection."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "φ:Z→Z/6Z, reduction modulo6", "steps": ["Kernel is6Z and the image is all residues"], "result": "Z/6Z is the quotient by6Z", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A quotient by a nonnormal subgroup is not a group quotient."], "pitfalls": ["A quotient by a nonnormal subgroup is not a group quotient.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-orbit-stabilizer"><data key="title">Orbit–stabilizer theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-orbit-stabilizer", "topicId": "math-group-actions", "title": "Orbit–stabilizer theorem", "latex": "|Gx|=|G|/|G_x|", "plain": "Orbit–stabilizer theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "G", "meaning": "finite acting group", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Gx", "meaning": "orbit", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Gx-subscript", "meaning": "stabilizer", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["A genuine group action is specified."], "derivation": ["Cosets of the stabilizer correspond bijectively to orbit points."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "S3 acts on{1,2,3}, x=1", "steps": ["Stabilizer has2 elements"], "result": "Orbit has3 points", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Orbit size depends on the action; the same abstract group has different orbits."], "pitfalls": ["Orbit size depends on the action; the same abstract group has different orbits.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-quotient-ring"><data key="title">Quotient ring well-definedness</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-quotient-ring", "topicId": "math-rings", "title": "Quotient ring well-definedness", "latex": "(a+I)(b+I)=ab+I", "plain": "Quotient ring well-definedness under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "R", "meaning": "ring", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "I", "meaning": "two-sided ideal", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a,b", "meaning": "ring elements", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["For noncommutative rings, a two-sided ideal is required."], "derivation": ["Changing representatives adds terms ai+jb+ij that remain in I."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "R=Z,I=5Z", "steps": ["(3+I)(4+I)=12+I"], "result": "Product is the residue2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["An additive subgroup need not be an ideal and may not support quotient multiplication."], "pitfalls": ["An additive subgroup need not be an ideal and may not support quotient multiplication.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-crt"><data key="title">Chinese remainder theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-crt", "topicId": "math-rings", "title": "Chinese remainder theorem", "latex": "R/(I\\cap J)\\cong R/I\\times R/J", "plain": "Chinese remainder theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "R", "meaning": "commutative ring with identity", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "I,J", "meaning": "ideals", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["I+J=R."], "derivation": ["Map a to its residue pair.", "Kernel is I∩J and comaximality gives surjectivity."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "R=Z,I=3Z,J=5Z", "steps": ["x=8 has residues(2,3)"], "result": "Z/15Z≅Z/3Z×Z/5Z", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Moduli4 and6 are not coprime, so arbitrary residue pairs need not lift."], "pitfalls": ["Moduli4 and6 are not coprime, so arbitrary residue pairs need not lift.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-hilbert-basis"><data key="title">Hilbert basis theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-hilbert-basis", "topicId": "math-noetherian", "title": "Hilbert basis theorem", "latex": "R\\text{ Noetherian}\\Rightarrow R[x]\\text{ Noetherian}", "plain": "Hilbert basis theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "R", "meaning": "commutative ring", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "formal variable", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Noetherian means every ideal is finitely generated."], "derivation": ["Control the ideals of leading coefficients and inductively reduce polynomial degrees."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "R=k a field", "steps": ["Every ideal of k[x] is principal by Euclidean division"], "result": "k[x] is Noetherian", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["The countably generated polynomial ring k[x1,x2,...] is not Noetherian."], "pitfalls": ["The countably generated polynomial ring k[x1,x2,...] is not Noetherian.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-ag"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-galois"><data key="title">Finite Galois correspondence</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-galois", "topicId": "math-fields", "title": "Finite Galois correspondence", "latex": "K\\subseteq E\\subseteq L\\longleftrightarrow\\operatorname{Gal}(L/E)", "plain": "Finite Galois correspondence under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "L/K", "meaning": "finite Galois extension", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "E", "meaning": "intermediate field", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The extension must be normal and separable."], "derivation": ["Fixed fields and stabilizing subgroups are inverse inclusion-reversing constructions."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "L=Q(√2),K=Q", "steps": ["Automorphisms send √2 to±√2"], "result": "Two subgroups correspond to Q and Q(√2)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A purely inseparable extension can have a trivial automorphism group despite being nontrivial."], "pitfalls": ["A purely inseparable extension can have a trivial automorphism group despite being nontrivial.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-fermat"><data key="title">Fermat small theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-fermat", "topicId": "math-congruences", "title": "Fermat small theorem", "latex": "a^{p-1}\\equiv1\\pmod p", "plain": "Fermat small theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "p", "meaning": "prime", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "integer not divisible by p", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Work in the multiplicative group of nonzero residues modulo p."], "derivation": ["Lagrange theorem gives element order dividing p-1."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "p=7,a=2", "steps": ["2^6=64=1+9*7"], "result": "Residue1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A composite modulus may have nonzero nonunits; this is not a general primality certificate."], "pitfalls": ["A composite modulus may have nonzero nonunits; this is not a general primality certificate.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-maschke"><data key="title">Maschke complete reducibility</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-maschke", "topicId": "math-finite-representations", "title": "Maschke complete reducibility", "latex": "V=W\\oplus W^\\prime", "plain": "Maschke complete reducibility under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "G", "meaning": "finite group", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "V", "meaning": "finite-dimensional representation over k", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "W", "meaning": "invariant subspace", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["char(k) does not divide |G|."], "derivation": ["Average any projection onto W over G to obtain an equivariant projection."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "G=C2 over R acts by diag(1,-1)", "steps": ["The two coordinate axes are invariant"], "result": "Direct sum of trivial and sign representations", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["In characteristic2 the representation of C2 can have a nontrivial Jordan block."], "pitfalls": ["In characteristic2 the representation of C2 can have a nontrivial Jordan block.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-schur"><data key="title">Schur lemma over an algebraically closed field</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-schur", "topicId": "math-finite-representations", "title": "Schur lemma over an algebraically closed field", "latex": "\\operatorname{End}_G(V)=kI", "plain": "Schur lemma over an algebraically closed field under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "V", "meaning": "nonzero finite-dimensional irreducible representation", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "k", "meaning": "algebraically closed field", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Endomorphisms commute with the group action."], "derivation": ["Kernel and image are invariant.", "Subtract an eigenvalue and use irreducibility to force a scalar."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "An irreducible complex1D representation", "steps": ["Every linear map is multiplication by a complex scalar"], "result": "Commutant consists of scalars", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Over R irreducible commutants may be isomorphic to C or H, not only real scalars."], "pitfalls": ["Over R irreducible commutants may be isomorphic to C or H, not only real scalars.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-gt"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-tensor"><data key="title">Universal property of tensor product</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-tensor", "topicId": "math-tensor", "title": "Universal property of tensor product", "latex": "\\operatorname{Hom}(V\\otimes W,U)\\cong\\operatorname{Bil}(V,W;U)", "plain": "Universal property of tensor product under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "V,W,U", "meaning": "vector spaces over one field", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Bil", "meaning": "bilinear maps", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The isomorphism is natural in all spaces."], "derivation": ["Quotient the free vector space on pairs by the bilinearity relations."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "V=W=R²,U=R, b(v,w)=v·w", "steps": ["Set L(ei⊗ej)=δij and extend linearly"], "result": "One linear map represents the bilinear dot product", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["v⊗w is not a pair with componentwise nonlinear operations."], "pitfalls": ["v⊗w is not a pair with componentwise nonlinear operations.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-leinster"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-homology"><data key="title">Homology of a chain complex</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-homology", "topicId": "math-chain-complexes", "title": "Homology of a chain complex", "latex": "H_n(C)=\\ker\\partial_n/\\operatorname{im}\\partial_{n+1}", "plain": "Homology of a chain complex under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "C", "meaning": "chain complex", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "∂n", "meaning": "boundary maps", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["∂n∂n+1=0 ensures boundaries are cycles."], "derivation": ["Form the quotient of cycles by the subspace or subgroup of boundaries."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Cellular chains of a circle over Q", "steps": ["One0-cell, one1-cell, both boundary maps zero"], "result": "H0≅Q,H1≅Q", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Without boundary-squared-zero, the displayed quotient may not be defined."], "pitfalls": ["Without boundary-squared-zero, the displayed quotient may not be defined.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hatcher"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-yoneda"><data key="title">Yoneda lemma</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-yoneda", "topicId": "math-yoneda", "title": "Yoneda lemma", "latex": "\\operatorname{Nat}(\\operatorname{Hom}(A,-),F)\\cong F(A)", "plain": "Yoneda lemma under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "C", "meaning": "locally small category", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "A", "meaning": "object", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "F", "meaning": "C→Set covariant functor", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Functor variance must match the representable shown."], "derivation": ["Evaluate a natural transformation at idA.", "Recover its component on f:A→X as F(f) applied to the chosen element."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "C=Set,F=Hom(B,-)", "steps": ["A natural transformation is composition with a map B→A"], "result": "Natural transformations correspond to Hom(B,A)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Reversing variance without reversing arrows changes the statement."], "pitfalls": ["Reversing variance without reversing arrows changes the statement.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-leinster"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-euler"><data key="title">Euler characteristic of a finite cell complex</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-euler", "topicId": "math-homology", "title": "Euler characteristic of a finite cell complex", "latex": "\\chi(X)=\\sum_k(-1)^k c_k=\\sum_k(-1)^k b_k", "plain": "Euler characteristic of a finite cell complex under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "ck", "meaning": "number of k-cells", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "bk", "meaning": "dimension of kth rational homology", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Finite CW complex; homology over a field for Betti numbers."], "derivation": ["Rank-nullity makes boundary dimensions cancel in the alternating sum."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A sphere with one0-cell and one2-cell", "steps": ["χ=1+1"], "result": "χ(S²)=2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["For a torus χ=0; genus cannot be ignored when using planar counting."], "pitfalls": ["For a torus χ=0; genus cannot be ignored when using planar counting.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hatcher"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-pythagoras"><data key="title">Pythagorean theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-pythagoras", "topicId": "math-metric", "title": "Pythagorean theorem", "latex": "c^2=a^2+b^2", "plain": "Pythagorean theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "a,b", "meaning": "perpendicular Euclidean side lengths", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "c", "meaning": "hypotenuse length", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Right angle in a Euclidean inner-product plane."], "derivation": ["Expand the squared norm of two orthogonal vectors."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "a=3,b=4", "steps": ["c²=9+16"], "result": "c=5", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["On a curved surface geodesic triangles do not generally satisfy this Euclidean formula."], "pitfalls": ["On a curved surface geodesic triangles do not generally satisfy this Euclidean formula.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-cosines"><data key="title">Law of cosines</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-cosines", "topicId": "math-trigonometry", "title": "Law of cosines", "latex": "c^2=a^2+b^2-2ab\\cos\\theta", "plain": "Law of cosines under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "a,b", "meaning": "positive Euclidean lengths", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "θ", "meaning": "included angle in radians", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "c", "meaning": "opposite side", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The angle and lengths refer to the same Euclidean triangle."], "derivation": ["Write c-vector=u-v and expand its inner product."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "a=b=1,θ=π/3", "steps": ["c²=2-2*(1/2)"], "result": "c=1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A diagram drawn in perspective does not supply the actual metric angle."], "pitfalls": ["A diagram drawn in perspective does not supply the actual metric angle.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-cauchy"><data key="title">Cauchy–Schwarz inequality</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-cauchy", "topicId": "math-inner-products", "title": "Cauchy–Schwarz inequality", "latex": "|\\langle x,y\\rangle|\\leq\\|x\\|\\|y\\|", "plain": "Cauchy–Schwarz inequality under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "x,y", "meaning": "inner-product vectors", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Positive-definite real or complex inner product; choose one conjugate-linearity convention."], "derivation": ["Minimize ||x-ty||² over scalar t, or handle y=0 separately."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "x=(1,1),y=(1,-1)", "steps": ["Inner product0 while both norms are√2"], "result": "0≤2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["An indefinite Lorentzian bilinear form is not an inner product and needs different inequalities."], "pitfalls": ["An indefinite Lorentzian bilinear form is not an inner product and needs different inequalities.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-minkowski"><data key="title">Triangle inequality from an inner product</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-minkowski", "topicId": "math-inner-products", "title": "Triangle inequality from an inner product", "latex": "\\|x+y\\|\\leq\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|", "plain": "Triangle inequality from an inner product under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "x,y", "meaning": "inner-product vectors", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Norm is induced by a positive-definite inner product."], "derivation": ["Expand the norm square and bound the real cross term by Cauchy–Schwarz."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "x=(3,0),y=(0,4)", "steps": ["||x+y||=5≤3+4"], "result": "5≤7", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Equality requires compatible directions; perpendicular vectors usually give strict inequality."], "pitfalls": ["Equality requires compatible directions; perpendicular vectors usually give strict inequality.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-stokes"><data key="title">Generalized Stokes theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-stokes", "topicId": "math-stokes", "title": "Generalized Stokes theorem", "latex": "\\int_Md\\omega=\\int_{\\partial M}\\omega", "plain": "Generalized Stokes theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "M", "meaning": "oriented smooth compact n-manifold with boundary", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "ω", "meaning": "smooth(n-1)-form", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "d", "meaning": "exterior derivative increasing form degree by one", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "operator"}, {"symbol": "∂M", "meaning": "boundary with induced outward-normal-first orientation", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Boundary has induced outward-normal-first orientation; compact support may replace compactness."], "derivation": ["Use a partition of unity and reduce in charts to the one-variable fundamental theorem."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "M=[0,1],ω=f(x)=x² as a0-form", "steps": ["∫0¹2x dx=f(1)-f(0)"], "result": "1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Omitting boundary terms or changing boundary orientation changes the answer."], "pitfalls": ["Omitting boundary terms or changing boundary orientation changes the answer.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hatcher"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-inverse"><data key="title">Local inverse function theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-inverse", "topicId": "math-multivariable", "title": "Local inverse function theorem", "latex": "D(f^{-1})(f(a))=[Df(a)]^{-1}", "plain": "Local inverse function theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "C1 map between open subsets of Rn", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "base point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["detDf(a)≠0; conclusion is local."], "derivation": ["Linearize f near a and solve the correction by a contraction argument."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f(x)=x+x³ near0", "steps": ["f′(0)=1"], "result": "A differentiable local inverse exists with derivative1 at0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["f(x)=x² has derivative0 at0 and is not locally invertible across0."], "pitfalls": ["f(x)=x² has derivative0 at0 and is not locally invertible across0.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-implicit"><data key="title">Implicit differentiation theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-implicit", "topicId": "math-multivariable", "title": "Implicit differentiation theorem", "latex": "Dg=-[D_yF]^{-1}D_xF", "plain": "Implicit differentiation theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "F", "meaning": "C1 map(x,y)→Rm", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "g", "meaning": "local solution y=g(x)", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["F(a,b)=0 and DyF(a,b) is invertible."], "derivation": ["Apply the inverse function theorem to(x,y)↦(x,F(x,y)), then differentiate the constraint."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "F(x,y)=x²+y²-1 near(0,1)", "steps": ["DyF=2y≠0; g′=-x/y"], "result": "g′(0)=0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["At(1,0) this graph in y over x is singular even though the circle is smooth."], "pitfalls": ["At(1,0) this graph in y over x is singular even though the circle is smooth.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-geodesic"><data key="title">Geodesic equation</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-geodesic", "topicId": "math-connections", "title": "Geodesic equation", "latex": "\\ddot x^k+\\Gamma^k_{ij}\\dot x^i\\dot x^j=0", "plain": "Geodesic equation under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "xk", "meaning": "local coordinates along an affinely parameterized curve", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Γ", "meaning": "Levi-Civita connection coefficients", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "g", "meaning": "smooth positive-definite Riemannian metric", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "tensor"}, {"symbol": "τ", "meaning": "affine curve parameter", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "coordinate"}, {"symbol": "i,j,k", "meaning": "coordinate indices; repeated i,j are summed", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Smooth Riemannian metric and a specified chart; affine parameter."], "derivation": ["Stationarize the energy functional, or require covariant acceleration zero."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Euclidean Cartesian coordinates with Γ=0", "steps": ["x¨=0"], "result": "Geodesics are straight lines at constant speed", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["In polar coordinates Γ is nonzero even for flat space; coefficients are not curvature."], "pitfalls": ["In polar coordinates Γ is nonzero even for flat space; coefficients are not curvature.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "The connection equation extends to Lorentzian metrics, but a timelike proper-time stationary path is not a Riemannian global minimum; global extremality is not asserted."], "sourceIds": ["math-tong-gr", "math-mit-surfaces"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "TongEq3.29 and MITtheorem38.1/corollary38.2 inspected; the atlas states the Riemannian version.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-gauss-bonnet"><data key="title">Gauss–Bonnet for closed surfaces</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-gauss-bonnet", "topicId": "math-curvature", "title": "Gauss–Bonnet for closed surfaces", "latex": "\\int_MK\\,dA=2\\pi\\chi(M)", "plain": "Gauss–Bonnet for closed surfaces under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "M", "meaning": "closed oriented embedded surface", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "g", "meaning": "induced positive-definite Riemannian metric", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "tensor"}, {"symbol": "K", "meaning": "Gaussian curvature", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "scalar"}, {"symbol": "dA", "meaning": "metric area element", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "χ(M)", "meaning": "Euler characteristic, an integer topological invariant", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "π", "meaning": "circle constant", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "constant"}], "conditions": ["Source-paired version: M is a compact oriented smooth surface embedded in R3, without boundary, carrying the induced Riemannian metric g.", "K is its Gaussian curvature and dA the metric area element; the abstract intrinsic extension is not separately source-audited here."], "derivation": ["Triangulate by small geodesic pieces and sum local angle excesses; interior edge terms cancel."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Unit round sphere: K=1,area=4π", "steps": ["χ(S²)=2"], "result": "4π=2π*2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A disk needs a boundary geodesic-curvature term."], "pitfalls": ["A disk needs a boundary geodesic-curvature term.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units."], "sourceIds": ["math-mit-surfaces"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Exact boundary theorem and general-domain formula inspected; closed embedded-surface corollary follows by empty boundary.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-hamilton"><data key="title">Hamiltonian vector field</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-hamilton", "topicId": "math-symplectic-forms", "title": "Hamiltonian vector field", "latex": "\\iota_{X_H}\\omega=dH,\\quad\\dot q=\\partial_pH,\\ \\dot p=-\\partial_qH", "plain": "Hamiltonian vector field under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "ω", "meaning": "nondegenerate closed2-form", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "H", "meaning": "smooth Hamiltonian", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "q,p", "meaning": "canonical coordinates", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "ι_X", "meaning": "interior contraction with vector field X", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "operator"}, {"symbol": "d", "meaning": "exterior derivative", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "operator"}], "conditions": ["Convention ω=Σdq∧dp and contraction in the first vector slot.", "Global flow additionally requires completeness, for example compact M; only the local vector-field equation is asserted without that condition."], "derivation": ["Nondegeneracy uniquely solves the contraction equation.", "In coordinates compare coefficients of dq and dp."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "H=(p²+q²)/2", "steps": ["qdot=p,pdot=-q"], "result": "H is constant along the flow", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["An arbitrary nonclosed2-form need not define a symplectic structure."], "pitfalls": ["An arbitrary nonclosed2-form need not define a symplectic structure.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units."], "sourceIds": ["math-cannas-symplectic"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Exact definition18.1 and preceding construction/flow argument inspected.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-de-rham"><data key="title">de Rham theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-de-rham", "topicId": "math-cohomology", "title": "de Rham theorem", "latex": "H^k_{dR}(M)\\cong H^k(M;\\mathbb R)", "plain": "de Rham theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "M", "meaning": "smooth manifold", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "HdreRham", "meaning": "closed forms modulo exact forms", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Use ordinary smooth de Rham theory on a paracompact smooth manifold."], "derivation": ["Integrate forms over smooth singular simplices; Stokes makes a cochain map; local Poincaré lemma plus gluing gives the isomorphism."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "M=S¹", "steps": ["The angular form has nonzero integral around the circle"], "result": "H1dR(S¹)≅R", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Closed does not imply globally exact on a noncontractible manifold."], "pitfalls": ["Closed does not imply globally exact on a noncontractible manifold.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hatcher"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-tangent-variety"><data key="title">Jacobian tangent space</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-tangent-variety", "topicId": "math-varieties", "title": "Jacobian tangent space", "latex": "T_pV=\\ker Df(p)", "plain": "Jacobian tangent space under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "V", "meaning": "affine algebraic set cut out by ideal generators f", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "p", "meaning": "point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Use generators of the defining ideal over the base field; this is the Zariski tangent space."], "derivation": ["A first-order infinitesimal displacement p+εv satisfies f(p)+εDf(p)v=0 modulo ε²."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "V:y²-x³=0 at(0,0)", "steps": ["Both partial derivatives vanish"], "result": "Tangent space dimension2 although curve dimension1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Tangent dimension can jump at singular points; do not infer smooth dimension from every Jacobian kernel."], "pitfalls": ["Tangent dimension can jump at singular points; do not infer smooth dimension from every Jacobian kernel.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-milne-ag"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-epsilon-limit"><data key="title">Epsilon–delta limit</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-epsilon-limit", "topicId": "math-sequences", "title": "Epsilon–delta limit", "latex": "\\forall\\epsilon&gt;0\\ \\exists\\delta&gt;0:\\ 0&lt;|x-a|&lt;\\delta\\Rightarrow|f(x)-L|&lt;\\epsilon", "plain": "Epsilon–delta limit under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "real function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "limit point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "L", "meaning": "proposed limit", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Quantifiers range over points in the domain approaching a."], "derivation": ["To prove a limit, construct δ for each ε and verify the implication."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f(x)=2x,a=3,L=6", "steps": ["Choose δ=ε/2"], "result": "limx→3 2x=6", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Choosing δ depending on x after observing x reverses the quantifier order."], "pitfalls": ["Choosing δ depending on x after observing x reverses the quantifier order.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-chain"><data key="title">Chain rule</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-chain", "topicId": "math-derivatives", "title": "Chain rule", "latex": "D(g\\circ f)(a)=Dg(f(a))Df(a)", "plain": "Chain rule under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f,g", "meaning": "differentiable maps on compatible open Euclidean domains", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Differentiability holds at a and f(a), respectively."], "derivation": ["Insert each first-order expansion and bound the composed remainder by o(||h||)."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f(x)=x²,g(u)=sin u", "steps": ["(sin x²)′=cos(x²)*2x"], "result": "Derivative2x cos(x²)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Existence of partial derivatives alone does not ensure total differentiability."], "pitfalls": ["Existence of partial derivatives alone does not ensure total differentiability.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-taylor"><data key="title">Taylor theorem with remainder</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-taylor", "topicId": "math-derivatives", "title": "Taylor theorem with remainder", "latex": "f(x)=\\sum_{k=0}^n\\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+R_{n+1}", "plain": "Taylor theorem with remainder under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "Cn+1 real function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "expansion point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["On the segment from a to x, |f(n+1)|≤M gives |R|≤M|x-a|^(n+1)/(n+1)!."], "derivation": ["Repeated fundamental-theorem integration gives an integral remainder and its bound."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f=exp,a=0,n=2,x=0.1", "steps": ["Approximation1+0.1+0.005=1.105; M=e^0.1"], "result": "Error at most e^0.1*0.001/6", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A smooth function need not equal its infinite Taylor series."], "pitfalls": ["A smooth function need not equal its infinite Taylor series.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-ftc"><data key="title">Fundamental theorem of calculus</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-ftc", "topicId": "math-riemann", "title": "Fundamental theorem of calculus", "latex": "\\frac{d}{dx}\\int_a^xf(t)dt=f(x)", "plain": "Fundamental theorem of calculus under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "continuous function on[a,b]", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "interior point", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Continuity at x is sufficient for the pointwise derivative conclusion."], "derivation": ["Bound the average integral difference by the local oscillation of f."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f(t)=t²,a=0", "steps": ["Integral=x³/3"], "result": "Derivative=x²", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["For merely integrable f the pointwise derivative need not exist everywhere."], "pitfalls": ["For merely integrable f the pointwise derivative need not exist everywhere.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-uniform-derivative"><data key="title">Differentiating a sequence of functions</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-uniform-derivative", "topicId": "math-continuity", "title": "Differentiating a sequence of functions", "latex": "f_n\\to f,\\quad f_n^\\prime\\to g\\text{ uniformly}\\Rightarrow f^\\prime=g", "plain": "Differentiating a sequence of functions under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "fn", "meaning": "C1 functions on compact interval", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "g", "meaning": "uniform derivative limit", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Assume fn(x0) converges at one point; derivative convergence is uniform."], "derivation": ["Integrate fn′ from x0 and pass through uniform convergence."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "fn(x)=x/n on[0,1]", "steps": ["fn′=1/n→0 uniformly"], "result": "Limit f=0 and f′=0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Uniform convergence of functions alone does not control derivatives: sin(nx)/n→0 but derivatives cos(nx) do not converge."], "pitfalls": ["Uniform convergence of functions alone does not control derivatives: sin(nx)/n→0 but derivatives cos(nx) do not converge.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-dct"><data key="title">Dominated convergence theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-dct", "topicId": "math-convergence", "title": "Dominated convergence theorem", "latex": "\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\to\\int f\\,d\\mu", "plain": "Dominated convergence theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "μ", "meaning": "measure", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "fn", "meaning": "measurable scalar functions", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "g", "meaning": "integrable dominating function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["fn→f almost everywhere and |fn|≤g almost everywhere for every n."], "derivation": ["Apply Fatou to g±fn, or use integrable tail control and convergence in measure on finite pieces."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "fn(x)=x^n on[0,1] with Lebesgue measure", "steps": ["Dominated by1; integral=1/(n+1)"], "result": "Integrals tend to0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["fn=n*1(0,1/n) tends to0 a.e. but has integral1; no fixed integrable domination."], "pitfalls": ["fn=n*1(0,1/n) tends to0 a.e. but has integral1; no fixed integrable domination.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units."], "sourceIds": ["math-tao"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Exact selected theorem statement and surrounding argument inspected; not all source proofs audited.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-fubini"><data key="title">Tonelli and Fubini</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-fubini", "topicId": "math-convergence", "title": "Tonelli and Fubini", "latex": "\\int_{X\\times Y}f=\\int_X\\!\\int_Yf=\\int_Y\\!\\int_Xf", "plain": "Tonelli and Fubini under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "X,Y", "meaning": "σ-finite measure spaces", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "f", "meaning": "measurable function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Nonnegative f permits Tonelli with infinity; signed/complex f needs absolute integrability for Fubini."], "derivation": ["Approximate nonnegative functions by simple functions; split absolutely integrable f into controlled parts."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f(x,y)=xy on[0,1]²", "steps": ["Each iterated integral=(1/2)*(1/2)"], "result": "1/4", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Conditionally convergent improper double integrals can depend on order."], "pitfalls": ["Conditionally convergent improper double integrals can depend on order.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-tao"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-radon"><data key="title">Radon–Nikodym theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-radon", "topicId": "math-measure", "title": "Radon–Nikodym theorem", "latex": "\\nu(A)=\\int_Ah\\,d\\mu", "plain": "Radon–Nikodym theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "μ,ν", "meaning": "σ-finite positive measures on one measurable space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "h", "meaning": "density", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["ν is absolutely continuous with respect to μ."], "derivation": ["Construct the density via maximal dominated integral or a Hilbert-space reduction; uniqueness is almost everywhere."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "ν(A)=∫A2x dx on[0,1]", "steps": ["h(x)=2x"], "result": "ν([0,1])=1", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A point mass is not absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure."], "pitfalls": ["A point mass is not absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-tao"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-riesz"><data key="title">Riesz representation in a Hilbert space</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-riesz", "topicId": "math-hilbert", "title": "Riesz representation in a Hilbert space", "latex": "\\ell(x)=\\langle x,y\\rangle", "plain": "Riesz representation in a Hilbert space under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "H", "meaning": "Hilbert space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "ℓ", "meaning": "bounded linear functional", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "y", "meaning": "representing vector", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Inner product is taken linear in its first argument; completeness is required."], "derivation": ["Use the orthogonal complement of kerℓ, normalize its generator and handle ℓ=0."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "H=R²,ℓ(x1,x2)=2x1-3x2", "steps": ["Choose y=(2,-3)"], "result": "Unique representation by y", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Unbounded linear functionals are outside the theorem."], "pitfalls": ["Unbounded linear functionals are outside the theorem.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-axler"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-banach"><data key="title">Contraction mapping theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-banach", "topicId": "math-banach", "title": "Contraction mapping theorem", "latex": "d(x_n,x_*)\\leq\\frac{q^n}{1-q}d(x_1,x_0)", "plain": "Contraction mapping theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "X", "meaning": "nonempty complete metric space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "T", "meaning": "X→X", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "q", "meaning": "contraction factor", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["d(Tx,Ty)≤q d(x,y) with0≤q&lt;1."], "derivation": ["Iterate T; geometric tail bounds make a Cauchy sequence; continuity gives a fixed point and contraction gives uniqueness."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "T(x)=(x+2)/2 on R", "steps": ["Fixed-point equation x=(x+2)/2"], "result": "Unique fixed point2", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["T(x)=x+1 is nonexpansive with q=1 but has no fixed point on R."], "pitfalls": ["T(x)=x+1 is nonexpansive with q=1 but has no fixed point on R.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Exact selected theorem statement and surrounding argument inspected; not all source proofs audited.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-compact-spectral"><data key="title">Compact self-adjoint spectral theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-compact-spectral", "topicId": "math-spectral-theorem", "title": "Compact self-adjoint spectral theorem", "latex": "Tx=\\sum_j\\lambda_j\\langle x,e_j\\rangle e_j", "plain": "Compact self-adjoint spectral theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "T", "meaning": "compact self-adjoint operator on Hilbert space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "ej", "meaning": "orthonormal eigenvectors for nonzero eigenvalues", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["The remaining orthogonal subspace is kerT; nonzero eigenvalues can accumulate only at0."], "derivation": ["Maximize the quadratic form on unit vectors, extract eigenvectors using compactness, then iterate on orthogonal complements."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "T on l² defined by(Tx)n=xn/n", "steps": ["Standard basis vectors have eigenvalues1/n"], "result": "Finite-rank truncations converge in operator norm", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["The identity on infinite-dimensional l² is self-adjoint but not compact."], "pitfalls": ["The identity on infinite-dimensional l² is self-adjoint but not compact.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-distribution"><data key="title">Weak derivative and the delta distribution</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-distribution", "topicId": "math-distributions", "title": "Weak derivative and the delta distribution", "latex": "\\langle Df,\\varphi\\rangle=-\\langle f,\\varphi^\\prime\\rangle", "plain": "Weak derivative and the delta distribution under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "φ", "meaning": "smooth compactly supported test function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "f", "meaning": "locally integrable function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Distributions act continuously on test functions; boundary terms vanish by compact support."], "derivation": ["Define the derivative by integration by parts and extend beyond ordinary derivatives."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Heaviside H=0 for x&lt;0,1 for x&gt;0", "steps": ["-∫0∞φ′(x)dx=φ(0)"], "result": "DH=δ0", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["δ0 is not an ordinary function with a finite point value."], "pitfalls": ["δ0 is not an ordinary function with a finite point value.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-plancherel"><data key="title">Plancherel identity</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-plancherel", "topicId": "math-harmonic-analysis", "title": "Plancherel identity", "latex": "\\|\\widehat f\\|_2=\\|f\\|_2", "plain": "Plancherel identity under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "L2 function on Rn", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "Fourier transform", "meaning": "unitarily normalized", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Use fhat(ξ)=(2π)^(-n/2)∫exp(-ix·ξ)f(x)dx initially on Schwartz functions."], "derivation": ["Parseval holds for rapidly decaying smooth functions; density uniquely extends the transform to L2."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Normalized Gaussian in one dimension", "steps": ["Its unitary Fourier transform is another Gaussian with the same L2 norm"], "result": "L2 energy is preserved", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A different Fourier normalization changes constants; an FFT bin is not automatically a physical spectral density."], "pitfalls": ["A different Fourier normalization changes constants; an FFT bin is not automatically a physical spectral density.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-picard"><data key="title">Picard–Lindelöf local theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-picard", "topicId": "math-ode", "title": "Picard–Lindelöf local theorem", "latex": "\\dot x=f(t,x),\\quad x(t_0)=x_0", "plain": "Picard–Lindelöf local theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "continuous time-dependent vector field", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "trajectory", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["f is locally Lipschitz in x uniformly on a suitable time neighborhood."], "derivation": ["The integral equation is a contraction on a small closed trajectory ball."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "xdot=x,x(0)=1", "steps": ["Integrate or separate variables"], "result": "Unique local and global solution e^t", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["xdot=√|x| at x0=0 has delayed nonunique solutions; continuity alone is insufficient."], "pitfalls": ["xdot=√|x| at x0=0 has delayed nonunique solutions; continuity alone is insufficient.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lebl"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-heat"><data key="title">Periodic heat semigroup</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-heat", "topicId": "math-heat", "title": "Periodic heat semigroup", "latex": "u_k(t)=e^{-\\kappa |k|^2t}u_k(0)", "plain": "Periodic heat semigroup under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "uk", "meaning": "Fourier coefficient on2π-periodic torus", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "κ", "meaning": "positive nondimensional diffusivity", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Initial data in L2; use periodic Laplacian with specified realization."], "derivation": ["Fourier expansion diagonalizes the Laplacian; solve each scalar ODE."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "u(x,0)=sin x,κ=1", "steps": ["Only modes±1 survive with decay e^-t"], "result": "u=e^-t sin x", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Backward heat evolution amplifies high frequencies and is not a stable inverse on L2."], "pitfalls": ["Backward heat evolution amplifies high frequencies and is not a stable inverse on L2.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-lax-milgram"><data key="title">Lax–Milgram weak existence</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-lax-milgram", "topicId": "math-variational", "title": "Lax–Milgram weak existence", "latex": "a(u,v)=\\ell(v)\\quad\\forall v\\in H", "plain": "Lax–Milgram weak existence under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "H", "meaning": "real Hilbert space", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "a", "meaning": "bounded coercive bilinear form", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "ℓ", "meaning": "bounded linear functional", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["a(v,v)≥α||v||² with α&gt;0; boundedness holds uniformly."], "derivation": ["Represent the form by a bounded operator; coercivity gives a bounded inverse using Hilbert-space arguments."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "H=R²,a(u,v)=u·v,ℓ(v)=(1,2)·v", "steps": ["Choose u=(1,2)"], "result": "Unique solution u=(1,2)", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A positive semidefinite form with a kernel can lose uniqueness."], "pitfalls": ["A positive semidefinite form with a kernel can lose uniqueness.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-ito"><data key="title">Itô formula for a scalar diffusion</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-ito", "topicId": "math-stochastic", "title": "Itô formula for a scalar diffusion", "latex": "df(X_t)=f^\\prime(X_t)b_tdt+f^\\prime(X_t)\\sigma_tdW_t+\\tfrac12f^{\\prime\\prime}(X_t)\\sigma_t^2dt", "plain": "Itô formula for a scalar diffusion under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "W", "meaning": "standard Brownian motion", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "X", "meaning": "Itô process", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "f", "meaning": "C2 scalar function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "b,σ", "meaning": "adapted coefficients", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Local integrability conditions define the stochastic integrals; localization handles unbounded coefficients."], "derivation": ["Taylor-expand increments; Brownian quadratic variation yields(dW)²=dt in the rigorous partition limit."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "X=W,f(x)=x²", "steps": ["df=2W dW+dt"], "result": "W_t²=2∫0^tW_s dW_s+t", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Ordinary chain rule misses the drift correction;(dW)²=dt is mnemonic, not algebra of infinitesimal numbers."], "pitfalls": ["Ordinary chain rule misses the drift correction;(dW)²=dt is mnemonic, not algebra of infinitesimal numbers.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-clt"><data key="title">IID central limit theorem</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-clt", "topicId": "math-limit-laws", "title": "IID central limit theorem", "latex": "\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\mu)}{\\sigma\\sqrt n}\\Rightarrow N(0,1)", "plain": "IID central limit theorem under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "Xi", "meaning": "independent identically distributed real variables", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "μ", "meaning": "finite mean", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "σ²", "meaning": "finite positive variance", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Finite variance0&lt;σ²&lt;∞; weak convergence concerns distributions, not every sample path."], "derivation": ["Expand the characteristic function near0 and raise it to the nth power."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Xi take±1 with equal probability", "steps": ["μ=0,σ=1; normalized sum has variance1"], "result": "Its distribution approaches standard normal", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Cauchy variables have no finite variance and do not satisfy this normalized theorem."], "pitfalls": ["Cauchy variables have no finite variance and do not satisfy this normalized theorem.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-hairer"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-condition"><data key="title">Linear-system perturbation bound</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-condition", "topicId": "math-floating-point", "title": "Linear-system perturbation bound", "latex": "\\frac{\\|\\delta x\\|}{\\|x\\|}\\leq\\kappa(A)\\frac{\\|\\delta b\\|}{\\|b\\|}", "plain": "Linear-system perturbation bound under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "A", "meaning": "invertible matrix", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x", "meaning": "A^-1b", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "κ(A)", "meaning": "||A||||A^-1|| in a subordinate norm", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Perturb only b, with b≠0; δx=A^-1δb."], "derivation": ["Bound ||δx|| by ||A^-1||||δb|| and use ||b||≤||A||||x||."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "A=diag(1,10^-6)", "steps": ["In the Euclidean norm κ=10^6"], "result": "Small data errors can amplify by up to one million in worst directions", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["Algorithmic backward stability does not make an ill-conditioned problem well-conditioned."], "pitfalls": ["Algorithmic backward stability does not make an ill-conditioned problem well-conditioned.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-dlmf-linear"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-newton"><data key="title">Local quadratic Newton convergence</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">theorem</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-newton", "topicId": "math-nonlinear-solvers", "title": "Local quadratic Newton convergence", "latex": "x_{n+1}=x_n-\\frac{f(x_n)}{f^\\prime(x_n)}", "plain": "Local quadratic Newton convergence under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "f", "meaning": "C2 scalar function", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "x*", "meaning": "simple root", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["f(x*)=0,f′(x*)≠0; initial iterate sufficiently close and derivative bounded away from0 locally."], "derivation": ["Taylor expansion at the root bounds the next error by a constant times the current error squared."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "f=x²-2,x0=1.5", "steps": ["x1=(1.5+2/1.5)/2=1.4166667"], "result": "Iterates approach√2 quadratically", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["At a multiple root, plain Newton is generally only linearly convergent."], "pitfalls": ["At a multiple root, plain Newton is generally only linearly convergent.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-dlmf-linear"], "status": "theorem", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-f-kernel"><data key="title">A proof judgment is relative to an environment</data><data key="kind">formula</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">conditional-result</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-f-kernel", "topicId": "math-proof-assistants", "title": "A proof judgment is relative to an environment", "latex": "\\Gamma\\vdash t:P", "plain": "A proof judgment is relative to an environment under the stated hypotheses.", "variables": [{"symbol": "Γ", "meaning": "declared axioms,definitions and typing context", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "t", "meaning": "proof term", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}, {"symbol": "P", "meaning": "formal proposition", "unit": "dimensionless (abstract mathematical convention)", "kind": "mathematical object"}], "conditions": ["Kernel checks the exact formal statement using its trusted rules; connection to an informal claim is an additional obligation."], "derivation": ["For reflexive equality the constructor rfl certifies a term equal to itself after permitted reduction."], "workedExample": {"inputs": "Lean-style statement:∀n:Nat,n+0=n", "steps": ["Unfold the recursion equation for addition at zero; use reflexivity"], "result": "An illustrative proof schema, not a Lean run executed for this atlas", "units": "dimensionless; no physical scale or empirical data assigned"}, "limitingCases": ["A kernel can correctly check a mistranslated statement or a theorem depending on an unwanted axiom."], "pitfalls": ["A kernel can correctly check a mistranslated statement or a theorem depending on an unwanted axiom.", "The proof and example apply to the stated structures and domain; a physical application needs an independently specified measurement map and units.", "Source audit limit: this is a teaching reconstruction; related references are not a separately verified exact theorem/proof pairing."], "sourceIds": ["math-lean"], "status": "conditional-result", "sourceAudit": "Non-source-audited teaching reconstruction: cited references supply related context, but an exact theorem-to-source passage was not independently checked.", "kind": "formula", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-lebl"><data key="title">Basic Analysis I</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-lebl", "title": "Basic Analysis I", "url": "https://www.jirka.org/ra/realanal.pdf", "authors": "Jiří Lebl", "year": 2026, "version": "Version6.3,15May2026", "kind": "source", "status": "selected-text", "readDepth": "Selected passages only: front matter and contents; uniform-continuity discussion; contraction-mapping theorem7.6.2 and beginning of proof. No whole-book or full-proof verification claimed.", "sectionsRead": ["front matter and contents", "uniform-continuity discussion", "contraction-mapping theorem7.6.2 and beginning of proof"], "claim": "Precise quantifiers, convergence hypotheses and complete-space fixed-point argument.", "limitations": "Only selected passages were inspected; displayed atlas proofs are independent reconstructions, not quotations.", "passageKind": "research-note", "note": "Atlas paraphrase and independent teaching reconstruction; this source is not an endorsement or a certification of every linked application.", "capture": "docs/field-atlas/sources/mathematics/math-lebl.pdf", "sha256": "9c204ae1f1ee1641fa604a6456c0d13db4390db44633ee2df9c9d97b5486b7a6", "retrievedAt": "2026-11-25T06:24:42.279956+00:00", "domain": "mathematics", "recordKind": "textbook", "sourceKind": "textbook"}</data></node><node id="math-axler"><data key="title">Linear Algebra Done Right, fourth edition</data><data key="kind">source</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">selected-text</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-axler", "title": "Linear Algebra Done Right, fourth edition", "url": "https://linear.axler.net/LADR4e.pdf", "authors": "Sheldon Axl