"title": "Optimality of Gerver's Sofa", "kind": "paper", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-paper-hilbert6"><data key="title">Hilbert's sixth problem: derivation of fluid equations via Boltzmann's kinetic theory</data><data key="kind">paper</data><data key="domain">mathematics</data><data key="status">selected preprint statements read; full proof not independently verified</data><data key="metadata">{"id": "math-paper-hilbert6", "sourceId": "math-hilbert6", "topicIds": ["math-hilbert-sixth"], "question": "Can specified fluid equations be obtained from deterministic elastic hard-sphere systems?", "method": "Controlled kinetic limits on2D/3D tori, then fluid scalings under source regularity/limit assumptions.", "result": "Source claims compressible Euler and incompressible Navier–Stokes–Fourier derivations in the declared program.", "whatIsNew": "Extends a mathematical kinetic-to-fluid derivation program; this does not prove arbitrary3D fluid regularity.", "limitations": "Not all of Hilbert6, arbitrary molecular potentials, global3D Navier–Stokes regularity or an arbitrary hydrodynamic regime. Theorem1 assumes an existing Boltzmann solution, velocity-weighted L∞ and initial-gradient bounds, and max(1,α)max(1,A)max(1,t_fin) ≪ sqrt(log|log ε|); fluid statements impose smooth limiting solutions and well-prepared data.", "status": "selected preprint statements read; full proof not independently verified", "equationsUsed": [], "replication": "No full proof reconstruction or formal verification performed by this atlas; selected theorem scope was compared with foundational definitions.", "nextQuestions": ["Inspect subsequent revisions and independent assessments after this dated corpus.", "Reconstruct the full theorem assumptions before transferring the result to a different setting."], "title": "Hilbert's sixth problem: derivation of fluid equations via Boltzmann's kinetic theory", "kind": "paper", "domain": "mathematics"}</data></node><node id="math-open-